    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you also touch on why developers sometimes complain that \"everything is breaking\" when they switch over to a more complex distributed system like horizontal scaling? Like, from an operational standpoint, why does it feel so much more unpredictable or \"messy\" than just running one big server?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The perception of things \"breaking\" in a distributed system often stems from the introduction of network latency and state management complexity. When you have one big server, all the data and processes are local, which is simple but brittle. With horizontal scaling, you are now dealing with multiple independent services communicating over a network, which introduces potential issues like network timeouts, inconsistent data across different nodes, or race conditions. It feels \"messy\" because you are no longer debugging one monolithic machine; you are debugging the *interactions* between many independent components.",
      "source": "target",
